    "saint-charbel-pilgrimage.html": {
      "The monastery receives visitors daily; check opening times with the monastery before you travel. It sits about 52 kilometers from Beirut - usually around ninety minutes by car, depending on traffic, up from the coastal city of Jbeil (Byblos). There is no regular public transport to the gates, so plan on a rental car, a taxi for the day, or an organized pilgrimage group.": 1,
      ": Check with the monastery for any current charges. Budget for transport, and for the candle or oil you may want to take home.": 1,
      "The monastery, the tomb, and the hermitage are open to everyone, of any faith or none. Check with the monastery before you travel for any current charges or opening times.": 1,
      "Spring through autumn for the mountain sites - winter snow can close the roads to Bekaa Kafra and the Cedars. Check with the monastery for current opening times.": 1,
      "Conditions change - check your government's current travel advice before booking, and take local advice on routes once you arrive. The pilgrimage sites themselves normally receive visitors; check before you go.": 1
    },
    "visit-annaya.html": {
      "A practical pilgrim's guide to the monastery where Saint Charbel lived, died, and lies entombed: how to get there, what to see and in what order, praying at the tomb, the hermitage, the holy oil, and the days that draw the crowds. Everyone is welcome.": 1,
      "The drive from Beirut is about 52 kilometers, usually around": 1,
      "depending on traffic: north along the coastal highway to Jbeil, then inland and uphill on a paved mountain road - about 17 kilometers from the coast - all the way to the monastery gates. There is": 1,
      "The monastery": 1,
      "receives visitors daily": 1,
      "; check opening times with the monastery before you travel. You do not need to be Catholic, or Christian, to enter and pray.": 1
    }
  }
